A||||8||Spektyr||Cleaning my motor.||||11||
Z||000000||Spektyr||04-21-2001||01:53 AM||||Man, if Pontiac didn't make the stock intake and valve covers a nice-looking shade of red!  Didn't realize mine were that color until I accidentally smudged some of the dirt off.  I cleared most of it with a dry rag, but now there's a clean spot, so I might as well do the whole thing.<P>What's the best method for cleaning a motor?  (I've never had a hot car, I just kept engines clean enough to notice oil leaks before).  I've seen those spray cleaners at the parts stores, but I don't really like the idea of just hosing a motor down with anything, especially something that says "WARNING, do not use on wiring, distributers or paint"... three things in abundance under the rear deck.<P>I mean, how lazy are we getting?  Cars don't wash themselves, and I'm certainly not above a little honest work to get a good-looking motor.  I'm thinking of just getting some dishwashing soap and water on a rag, wringing it out and applying some elbow grease.<P>So is there a 'right' way to do this?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||11
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-21-2001||07:39 AM||||Hey Spektyr!<BR>Here is my way of cleaning the engine compartment...<BR>Drive down to the quarter car wash and pressure wash it!<BR>Be sure the engine is RUNNING!!!<BR>Have fun!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||jaguiler||04-21-2001||08:32 AM||||Careful with cold water hit hot pipes...."cracked manifolds" aslo watch out for the electrical.  The engine cleaner from the parts store work really well.  I prefer using them.  I just cover up the distributor with a plastic bag spray the foamy spray over anything that looks metal, painted, or even plastic, and let it sit for 5 mintues.  Then scrub the tough spots.  Then rinse from the TOP down.  High pressure may force the water in places where you don't want it. So use a garden hose.....  Good luck...<BR> <P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||04-21-2001||09:08 AM||||I have used both the above methods. A product called GUNK works well on a WARM engine.  Cover the distributor, spray the GUNK on, let it sit, then hose it off. Restart the car to have it heat up and evaporate the excess water.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jay||04-21-2001||09:13 AM||||also be careful of the alternator.  I had mine go once right after I cleaned the motor.  Can't be sure if it was the engine degreaser or if it was already on its way out but, better safe than sorry.||||reg||1
Z||000005||bubbajoexx||04-21-2001||12:51 PM||||use good old waterless hand cleaner and apply it to the engine with an old paint brush wait ten minits and was off with a hose leave motor cold dose not need to be hot and all the grease and grim will was right off||||reg||10
Z||000006||wkayl||04-21-2001||02:00 PM||||Also cover the EGR solenoid (the thing with vacune lines above water pump near the antifreeze filler).  I power washed my engine and ended up fighting the dreaded "code 32" until I replaced it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||04-21-2001||04:34 PM||||I see in the Fiero On-line Service guide, someone reccomends spraying karosine(sp?) on everything. Any problems with that? I wash my engine every weekend with that foamy GUNK Engine Brite or whatever, then spray the engine and compartment with WD-40 or other spray oil products. I would think Karosine would really get that thing clean. <P>P.S. I spray the distributor also, and have never had a problem at all. Car always starts right up. Just don't hold the water on it for an extrended period of time and you should be fine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||04-21-2001||08:18 PM||||COLD MOTOR CLEANING ONLY! never spray a hot motor. good way to crack the exhaust manifolds.<P>Citrisolve, GOJO or any other Orange/citrus based cleaner. (Even the cirtus hand cleaners with pumace will do.) Work it in with a brush on the heavy stuff. This won't hurt plug wires and most other plastic parts if it gets on them. (Always keep cleaners out of the alternator... You don't want to risk washing the lube out of the bearings or contaminating the brushes.) Also it won't contaminate the belts and shorten the life of them like petrolium based cleaners can.||||reg||1
